   "link": null,
   "locked": false
  },
  {
   "id": "hld-text-9",
   "type": "text",
   "x": 1038.4,
   "y": 1776.0,
   "width": 644,
   "height": 375.0,
   "angle": 0,
   "strokeColor": "#1864ab",
   "backgroundColor": "transparent",
   "fillStyle": "solid",
   "strokeWidth": 2,
   "strokeStyle": "solid",
   "roughness": 0,
   "opacity": 100,
   "groupIds": [],
   "frameId": null,
   "roundness": null,
   "seed": 80521325,
   "version": 1,
   "versionNonce": 184570286,
   "isDeleted": false,
   "boundElements": [],
   "updated": 1,
   "link": null,
   "locked": false,
   "text": "STEP BY STEP (numbers match the badges)\n1. You run a command; click calls the matching function.\n2. attach / detach capture nothing themselves: they store the request (a\n    container name or a label) in SQLite.\n3. A running `watch` daemon reads those requests on its next pass (within 5s) -\n    that's how one terminal steers a daemon running in another.\n4. watch builds a CaptureManager (owns the tcpdumps) and a Selector (decides what\n    to capture), and first kills any tcpdump a crashed earlier run left behind.\n5. It then runs 4 things side by side: the controller loop, the enrich loop that\n    turns pcaps into rows, a port sampler for host-network containers, and a\n    Docker-events listener.\n6. sidecar runs inside the target's network namespace. It checks eth0 exists and\n    refuses to start if the namespace is really the host's (a --network host\n    target).\n7. Its own 1-second loop keeps the capture healthy, and exits with code 3 when\n    the target stops, so the problem shows up in `docker ps`.\n8. sidecars starts a Supervisor that manages sidecar containers through the\n    Docker API.\n9. Read commands open the database, run a query from analyze.py / report.py,\n    print, and exit.",
   "originalText": "STEP BY STEP (numbers match the badges)\n1. You run a command; click calls the matching function.\n2. attach / detach capture nothing themselves: they store the request (a\n    container name or a label) in SQLite.\n3. A running `watch` daemon reads those requests on its next pass (within 5s) -\n    that's how one terminal steers a daemon running in another.\n4. watch builds a CaptureManager (owns the tcpdumps) and a Selector (decides what\n    to capture), and first kills any tcpdump a crashed earlier run left behind.\n5. It then runs 4 things side by side: the controller loop, the enrich loop that\n    turns pcaps into rows, a port sampler for host-network containers, and a\n    Docker-events listener.\n6. sidecar runs inside the target's network namespace. It checks eth0 exists and\n    refuses to start if the namespace is really the host's (a --network host\n    target).\n7. Its own 1-second loop keeps the capture healthy, and exits with code 3 when\n    the target stops, so the problem shows up in `docker ps`.\n8. sidecars starts a Supervisor that manages sidecar containers through the\n    Docker API.\n9. Read commands open the database, run a query from analyze.py / report.py,\n    print, and exit.",
   "fontSize": 15,
   "fontFamily": 2,
   "textAlign": "left",
   "verticalAlign": "middle",
   "containerId": "hld-rectangle-8",
   "lineHeight": 1.25,
   "autoResize": true
  },
  {
